&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PopTalks Episode 1 launches September 14.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Listen now on &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/poptalks-with-dr-tho%E1%BA%A1i-ng%C3%B4/id6802360545" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Apple Podcasts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;, &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://open.spotify.com/show/0MW6gMe065Fus09b1Zx7vE" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Spotify&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;, or &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLeKUIzZ-bTiw" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;YouTube&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Four years after the Supreme Court overturned&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Roe v. Wade&lt;/em&gt;, what does the path forward for reproductive rights in America actually look like?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the first episode of the new&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;PopTalks podcast&lt;/strong&gt;, Dr. Thoai Ngo sits down with&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Brigitte Amiri, Deputy Director of the ACLU&amp;rsquo;s Reproductive Freedom Project&lt;/strong&gt;, who has spent more than two decades litigating some of the country&amp;rsquo;s most consequential reproductive rights cases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their conversation goes beyond the courts to examine the landscape after&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Dobbs&lt;/em&gt;: abortion bans, medication abortion, stigma, dignity, and what it will take to build reproductive rights that can endure political change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As Amiri explains:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;I think one of the reasons why we got to the point of Dobbs is because abortion was so shamed and so stigmatized that it was easy for the Supreme Court to treat it as something other.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What comes next? Amiri is clear-eyed about the challenges ahead&amp;mdash;and equally clear about what it will take to build more durable reproductive rights.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PopTalks&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;brings leading scientists, policymakers, advocates, innovators, and community leaders into candid conversations about the forces shaping population health and our collective well-being.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Episode 1 launches September 14. You&amp;rsquo;re invited to listen&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://nam02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.publichealth.columbia.edu%2Facademics%2Fdepartments%2Fpopulation-family-health%2Fnews-events%2Fpoptalks&amp;amp;data=05%7C02%7Cca176%40cumc.columbia.edu%7C76d18a449405487b5cac08df0dd13f5f%7Cb0002a9b0017404d97dc3d3bab09be81%7C0%7C0%7C639244864504869316%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;amp;sdata=8EEelogzxNh2ADGUr1Cbwzhstts%2FthUrN6GDAoX%2Bgdw%3D&amp;amp;reserved=0" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s get into it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scientific and environmental data from the government, universities, and private organizations is often inaccessible and opaque. This is especially true for local residents from marginalized and disempowered communities, who have been historically disproportionately impacted by environmental pollution. There is a need, then, to make this data more understandable and useful for local residents interested in protecting their own environmental health.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This talk will outline a three-year partnership aimed at addressing this scientific data problem through the development of an AI-assisted framework that collects local water quality information, connects it to health outcomes and local concerns, and communicates it in language shaped by local community members themselves. Speakers will also discuss the water problems facing the Newark community and situate this water crisis within Newark&amp;rsquo;s broader environmental history.&lt;/p&gt;
