&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for the opening reception of the exhibit &lt;em&gt;Selected Prints&lt;/em&gt; by Radomir Đukanović.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Selected Prints&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;presents a focused selection of Radomir Đukanović&amp;rsquo;s graphic works produced over the past two decades, offering an overview of an artistic practice that moves fluidly between abstraction, gesture, and symbolic suggestion. Working through printmaking as a field of reduction, layering, and repetition, Đukanović constructs images that evoke landscapes, architectural fragments, organic forms, traces of writing, dreamscapes, everyday objects, and archaeological surfaces without resolving into fixed narratives. His restrained yet dynamic visual language places contemporary graphic practice in dialogue with the legacies of Art Informel, lyrical abstraction, the material poetics of Antoni T&amp;agrave;pies, the sign-based poetics of Paul Klee, and the experimental traditions of the Yugoslav neo-avant-garde.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The exhibited prints reveal the breadth of this vocabulary. Several compositions are structured around dense red and black linear networks that recall both architectural scaffolding and gestural drawing, contemporary wire fencing and the interwoven ornament of medieval&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;pletenie sloves&lt;/em&gt;, set against pale grounds that emphasize the tension between order and improvisation. Others reduce the image to simplified geometric forms or isolated chromatic fields, while a number of works introduce handwritten marks, repeated motifs, or rhythmic patterns that oscillate between text and image. Throughout the exhibition, richly textured surfaces, fragmented signs, and carefully balanced compositions invite prolonged viewing, encouraging the viewer to navigate between material presence and symbolic openness rather than narrative certainty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Historian Thomas W. Laqueur invites us to explore why dogs feature so prominently in the ways in which we picture ourselves and our stories.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;It is difficult to think of many other books that are at once so brilliant, so wonderfully entertaining, and so moving &amp;hellip;&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Dog&amp;rsquo;s Gaze&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;is full of exuberant insights about our canine friends, about art, and about the human condition.&amp;rdquo; &amp;mdash;Stephen Greenblatt&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Long before the phrase man&amp;rsquo;s best friend became common parlance, dogs were already standing beside us in art as in life. In&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Dog&amp;rsquo;s Gaze&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, the historian Thomas W. Laqueur invites us to explore why they feature more than any other animal in the ways in which we picture ourselves and our stories.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dogs have been ubiquitous in the worldmaking of visual artists as far back as the Paleolithic age. Looking across the Western tradition, from Giotto to Goya and Rubens to Rego, Laqueur shows what their presence&amp;mdash;as hunting partners, beloved friends, and even conduits to the afterlife&amp;mdash;reveals about our own ways of seeing and how we want to be remembered. Far from being mere motifs, dogs are an integral and intentional element of the images in which they appear: They provide narrative coherence; they look out and bear witness, often on the artist&amp;rsquo;s behalf; they illuminate our understanding of morality and melancholy and some, like us, become celebrities. Indeed, as Laqueur reveals, dogs in art are our social doppelg&amp;auml;ngers, our companions in looking and being.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Dog&amp;rsquo;s Gaze&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a unique visual history that examines the remarkable social bond between two species, shedding new light on the human condition through the eyes of our canine companions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thomas Laqueur&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;is the Helen Fawcett Distinguished Professor of History Emeritus at the University of California, Berkeley. He writes about the cultural history of modern Europe and has published books on topics ranging from working class religion and education during the industrial revolution to the history of the body&amp;mdash;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Making Sex: Body and Gender from the Greeks to Freud&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Work of the Dead: A Cultural History of Mortal Remains&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;,&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;for example. His most recent book,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Dog&amp;rsquo;s Gaze: A Visual History&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;(Penguin, 2026), from which this lecture is drawn, appeared in May. Laqueur is a founding editor of the journal&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Representations&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;and writes regularly for the&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;London Review of Books&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Threepenny Review&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, as well as for other journals. His work has been translated into twenty languages.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;This event is organized by the Maison Francaise with funding from the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://maisonfrancaise.columbia.edu/content/director-eugene-j-sheffer" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Eugene Sheffer&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;nbsp;Distinguished Speaker series, which honors eminent scholars whose work has had an important impact on their field of study. This series was created by the family of Eugene Sheffer to honor his commitment to the Columbia Maison Fran&amp;ccedil;aise, which he directed from 1942 to 1966.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;This event is co-sponsored by the Department of History, ICLS, the Department of Art History, and the Department of History &amp;amp; Archeology.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

