&lt;p&gt;21st Century C++&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Abstract: Why did C++ succeed on a massive scale without marketing or a rich owner? Its imminent death was frequently predicted even from before it was called C++. The reason is that C++ has some key C++ features that serve widespread needs, are manageable by &amp;ldquo;ordinary developers&amp;rdquo;, and are stable over decades. Here, I present C++ as a coherent whole where strongly-typed generic programming has a central role, where code is presented as modules, resources are never leaked, and error-handling is systematic. Code written along these lines tend to be smaller, faster, more maintainable, and more reliable than code reflecting 20th-century thinking. The features I present are ISO standard C++ and shipping in major compilers. This style of C++ is a logical development of the foundational principles from the earliest days. I will briefly explain how to avoid getting stuck in 20th-century C++ and describe the plans for &amp;ldquo;Profiles&amp;rdquo; to manage the complexity accreted over decades of serious use. Along the way, I&amp;rsquo;ll try to put the evolution of C++ in its historical context.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bio: Bjarne Stroustrup is the designer and original implementer of C++ as well as the author of The C++ Programming Language (4th Edition), A Tour of C++ (3rd edition), Programming: Principles and Practice using C++ (3rd Edition), and many popular and academic publications. Dr. Stroustrup is a professor at Columbia University in New York City. He is a member of the US National Academy of Engineering, and an IEEE, ACM, and CHM fellow. He received the 2018 Charles Stark Draper Prize, the IEEE Computer Society&amp;#39;s 2018 Computer Pioneer Award, and the 2017 IET Faraday Medal. His research interests include distributed systems, design, programming techniques, software development tools, and programming languages. He is actively involved in the ISO standardization of C++. He holds a masters in Mathematics from Aarhus University, where he is an honorary professor, and a PhD in Computer Science from Cambridge University, where he is an honorary fellow of Churchill College.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Background&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At a time of profound global political, social and technological change, human rights practitioners are confronting increasingly complex challenges. International human rights standards remain essential, but practitioners are also working in environments where civic space is shrinking, political commitments are contested, accountability mechanisms are under pressure, and communities affected by violence, exploitation, discrimination and repression are demanding greater agency in shaping the responses that affect their lives. The question is therefore not only what human rights standards exist, but how practitioners translate those standards into meaningful change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;12:00pm &lt;/strong&gt;Opening remarks&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;12:15&amp;ndash;1:00pm &lt;/strong&gt;PANEL 1&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;From Voice to Movement: Building Power for Rights and Equality&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Panelists:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ruchira Gupta&lt;/strong&gt;, Apne Aap Women Worldwide; Emmy-winning documentarian, author of &lt;em&gt;I Kick and I Fly&lt;/em&gt;; Professor, New York University&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gaisu Yari&lt;/strong&gt;, Advocacy and Policy Manager, Afghanistan Initiative, Malala Fund&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tsitsi Matekaire&lt;/strong&gt;, Director, Regions, Equality Now&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What does it take to turn lived experience, advocacy and public mobilisation into lasting human rights change for women and girls? This conversation will bring together practitioners working across women&amp;#39;s and girls&amp;#39; rights, education, gender equality, human trafficking, and democratic participation and grassroots organising. The panel will explore how movements are built and sustained; how practitioners connect individual experiences to systemic injustice; and how advocacy can translate into changes in law, policy, institutions and public attitudes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1:00&amp;ndash;1:30pm &lt;/strong&gt;Light lunch will be served&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1:30&amp;ndash;2:15pm &lt;/strong&gt;PANEL 2&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;From Evidence to Accountability: The Practitioner&amp;rsquo;s Toolkit for Justice&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Panelists:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kevin Hyland&lt;/strong&gt;, Chair of G20 IF20 Working Group on Human Trafficking&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;C&amp;eacute;line Bardet&lt;/strong&gt;, Founder and President, We are NOT Weapons of War&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Samer Khwaiss&lt;/strong&gt;, Executive Director and Founder, Black Basalt Foundation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;How do practitioners turn evidence of human rights violations into accountability, prevention and justice? This conversation will focus on the practical dimensions of documenting violations, understanding patterns of abuse, supporting victims and survivors, strengthening investigations and ensuring that evidence can contribute to accountability.&lt;/p&gt;

