&lt;p&gt;Massive, moving, and marked by fresh insights, the first comprehensive exhibition on Raphael in the U.S., &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Raphael: Sublime Poetry &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;(which was at The Metropolitan Museum of Art earlier this year) was a masterpiece of scholarship and diplomacy by its curator, &lt;strong&gt;Carmen C. Bambach.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;She brought together a display of 237 works, including some of the artist&amp;rsquo;s greatest masterpieces and lesser-known treasures, to tell a new story of Raphael&amp;rsquo;s talents.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Bambach is an expert in such achievements: she is the acclaimed Renaissance scholar who curated two earlier blockbusters, &lt;em&gt;Leonardo da Vinci: Master Draftsman &lt;/em&gt;in 2003 and &lt;em&gt;Michelangelo: Divine Draftsman and Designer &lt;/em&gt;in 2017&amp;ndash;18.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a conversation with Columbia Professor &lt;strong&gt;Michael Cole&lt;/strong&gt;, she will look back at the planning and the triumphant execution of this year&amp;rsquo;s &lt;a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/exhibitions/raphael-sublime-poetry" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;exhibition&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, which was seen by more than 562,000 visitors over its three-month run.&lt;/p&gt;
