&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Making Critical Mineral Recovery Bankable And Insurable: Breakfast Panel, Discussion, Networking Session&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Approval Required&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;Your registration is subject to host approval.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://luma.com/vc54cz1h" target="_blank"&gt;Request to Join&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;​&lt;strong&gt;Hosted by&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​&lt;a href="https://ccsi.columbia.edu/?utm_source=luma" target="_blank"&gt;Columbia Center on Sustainable Investment&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.carbontrust.com/en-as?utm_source=luma" target="_blank"&gt;Carbon Trust&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.solas.capital/?utm_source=luma" target="_blank"&gt;Solas Capital&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.ellenmacarthurfoundation.org/?utm_source=luma" target="_blank"&gt;Ellen MacArthur Foundation&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.buckstop.com/?utm_source=luma" target="_blank"&gt;Buckstop&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;​&lt;strong&gt;Who should join us&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​This conversation is built for lenders and insurers, and for the institutional investors, valuation professionals, asset owners, and corporate supply chain leaders who would put these models to work and can speak to what constrains them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;​&lt;strong&gt;What to expect&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​Speakers from Columbia Center on Sustainable Investment, Carbon Trust, Solas Capital, Ellen Macarthur Foundation, and Buckstop will lead the discussion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​A short keynote to set the stage, will be followed by a panel built around three focused questions on bankability, live audience questions, and blended networking at themed tables. The session operates under the Chatham House Rule.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​Participants will come away with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​A clear view of how circular models for critical minerals can become bankable and insurable in practice.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​Key insights on integrating residual value and end-of-life recovery into mainstream financing, risk, and portfolio frameworks for lenders, insurers, and institutional investors.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​Registration is confirmed by the organizers to balance the room across our audiences and among Community of Practice members and non-members. Breakfast is provided; dietary requirements are collected at registration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;​&lt;strong&gt;Program&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;​The global transition to clean energy faces acute critical mineral shortages, yet billions of dollars in recoverable materials remain trapped within deployed assets and discarded as waste due to a critical &amp;quot;bankability gap.&amp;quot; Because lenders and insurers cannot currently track, value, or underwrite these embedded materials, their residual value is entirely absent from balance sheets and borrowing bases. To overcome this bottleneck, this event convenes leading financial institutions, insurers, and circularity practitioners to explore practical solutions like asset residual value pricing and Metals-as-a-Service (MaaS). MaaS is a circular business model where retained ownership enables precise material tracking and generates stable, long-term cash flows. By bringing together diverse perspectives across the value chain, the panel will identify the specific term-sheet structures, risk-return profiles, and valuation methods required to transform critical mineral recovery from a financial blind spot into a fully investable and insurable asset class, drawing on live experience in structuring long-tenor, cash flow&amp;ndash;backed private debt for circular and energy-efficiency assets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Evolving Landscape of Climate Litigation: U.S. and Global Perspectives&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;REGISTER&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://connect.vancecenter.org/l/1106653/2026-08-24/2vs21sx" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HERE&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event, co-hosted by the Vance Center for International Justice and the Sabin Center for Climate Change Law at Columbia Law School, brings together leading voices in climate litigation for a conversation on where the field stands and where it&amp;rsquo;s headed. The first panel examines the U.S. landscape, where courts are confronting an escalating set of disputes&amp;mdash;from state and municipal claims against fossil fuel companies to federal efforts to preempt state-level litigation and address deregulation under the Trump administration. The second panel turns to global developments, exploring how courts around the world are applying international human rights and climate obligations, and what happens when litigation succeeds&amp;mdash;from implementation to lasting impact. A coffee break between panels will offer time for informal discussion among speakers and attendees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;El Ni&amp;ntilde;o 2026: How Big Could It Get?&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Muhammad Azhar Ehsan, Associate Research Scientist at the Center for Climate Systems Research (CCSR), Columbia Climate School&lt;/strong&gt;, for the next&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;ENSO &amp;amp; Climate Quarterly Briefing&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;as we examine the rapidly evolving&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;El Ni&amp;ntilde;o 2026&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;event.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;El Ni&amp;ntilde;o conditions have strengthened significantly during 2026, and current observations and forecasts suggest that the event is approaching the period when ENSO typically reaches its peak intensity. With a high probability of a strong&amp;mdash;and potentially very strong&amp;mdash;El Ni&amp;ntilde;o, the coming months could bring significant shifts in weather and climate patterns across many parts of the world.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What does a high-intensity El Ni&amp;ntilde;o mean for the upcoming seasons? Which regions may face increased risks of drought, heavy rainfall, flooding, heat, or other climate extremes?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This briefing will provide an overview of the latest ENSO observations and forecasts and explore the potential implications for weather and climate around the world.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Join us in person at Hogan Hall or online via Zoom.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Zoom:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;https://columbiauniversity.zoom.us/j/93055218447?pwd=mVbWqyTCdGgRcqYPrbIWyL6QlbmPV0.1&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The energy transition depends on the science and engineering that makes electrochemistry deployable at scale &amp;mdash; from mines and feedstocks through grids, chemicals and materials, and back into circulation. Batteries have to be safe, manufacturable, and affordable at grid scale. Electrolyzers have to compete on cost with incumbents built over a century. Critical minerals, commodity chemicals, and advanced materials have to move from feedstocks to purified products without recreating the environmental burdens we&amp;#39;re trying to escape. The CEEC Fall Symposium gathers CEEC faculty, PhDs, postdocs, and industry practitioners working across this arc. Two afternoon sessions &amp;mdash; Electrifying Chemicals and Materials, and The Grid Frontier &amp;mdash; pair CEEC faculty keynotes with lightning talks and industry panels focused on the technical and commercial problems that matter most. The day closes in Carleton Commons with a reception and poster session showcasing CEEC research and the commercialization pathways emerging from it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is part of &lt;a href="https://www.engineering.columbia.edu/about/events/climate-week-columbia-engineering-2026" target="_blank"&gt;Climate Week at Columbia Engineering 2026&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
